# Google Drive sync credentials. Committed as a template only —
# copy to `.env.local` and fill it in there. `.env.local` is git-ignored.
#
#   cp .env.local.example .env.local
#
# Without these the app builds and runs exactly as before; Settings → Sync simply
# doesn't offer Google Drive, and the boot screen doesn't offer "FROM GOOGLE DRIVE".
# Nothing in the test suite needs them: `?drive=test:<slot>` stands in for Google.
#
# Console setup, once (see specs/Technical-Specification.md §5.5):
#
#   1. console.cloud.google.com → new project → enable the Google Drive API.
#   2. OAuth consent screen → External. Scope: .../auth/drive.file, and nothing else.
#   3. PUBLISH TO PRODUCTION. drive.file is a non-sensitive scope, so this is
#      self-service with no verification review — and it is not optional: while the
#      app sits in "Testing", Google expires refresh tokens after 7 days, which is
#      the whole reason we use the authorization-code flow.
#   4. Credentials → OAuth client ID → Web application. Authorized redirect URIs:
#        http://localhost:5173/oauth-result/gdrive.html   (npm run dev)
#        http://localhost:5199/oauth-result/gdrive.html   (Playwright's server)
#        https://<your-host>/oauth-result/gdrive.html     (production, if any)
#
# The secret is not a secret here. A browser client cannot keep one, and Google's
# own guidance is to embed it; it exists because the code flow needs it to return a
# refresh token. The grant it can produce reaches nothing but the encrypted vault
# file this app created in your Drive.

VITE_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
VITE_GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=
